<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support. Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify weather-related words in simple text instructions (e.g., matching "sun" to a picture of the sun). Can match simple weather-related words to corresponding pictures (e.g., matching the word "sunny" to a picture of a sunny day). Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound in familiar words related to weather (e.g., identifying the sound of the letter /r/ in the word "rainy" when shown a picture of raindrops).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, class!" and displays large flashcards of different weather conditions (sun, rain, clouds, wind). The teacher holds up a flashcard, points to the word, and slowly reads it aloud (e.g., "sunny"). Students repeat chorally. The teacher then asks, "What is the weather today?" and points to the "sunny" flashcard, eliciting "It is sunny!" The teacher emphasizes the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "rainy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "rainy" and a picture of rain. The teacher clearly pronounces "rainy," highlighting the /r/ sound. The teacher then shows a simple written direction, "Draw rain." and models reading the instruction aloud while pointing to each word, then drawing a simple rain on a whiteboard. This demonstrates how to read and follow a one-step instruction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a sun?" (pointing to a cloud picture) - Expected answer: "No!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound is this?" (pointing to the letter 'R' in the word 'rainy' on a flashcard) - Expected answer: "/r/!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large chart or projector slide with simple weather pictures and corresponding one-word labels (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and short sentences: "It is sunny." "It is raining." "It is cloudy." "It is windy." The chart also includes simple instructions with visuals, e.g., "Match the sun" (with a picture of a sun), "Color the cloud" (with a picture of a cloud).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Can identify weather-related words in simple text instructions and match simple words to corresponding pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture of a rainy day and asks, "What is the weather today?" then points to the text "It is raining." Students chorally read the sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of the sun and asks, "Where is the word 'sun'?" Students point to the word "sun" on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads a simple instruction like "Circle the rain." and asks students to find and point to the word "rain" on the chart.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will read and match weather words (sun, rain, clouds, wind) to pictures. They will practice identifying phonemes /s/ and /r/ in words like "sunny" and "rainy". They will read simple descriptive sentences like "It is sunny." and "It is raining."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Matching):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with two columns. Column A has pictures of weather (sun, rain, cloud, wind). Column B has the corresponding weather words (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) in a jumbled order. Students draw lines to match each picture to its word. The teacher circulates, offering help and checking for correct matches.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Circle the Word):</b> The teacher distributes another worksheet. Each item has a picture of a weather condition (e.g., a picture of a sunny day) followed by two simple words (e.g., "sunny" and "rainy"). Students read the words and circle the word that matches the picture. This reinforces reading comprehension at the word level and phonemic awareness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Guided Reading & Action - "Read and Act"):</b> The teacher shows flashcards with very simple written instructions (e.g., "It is sunny. Put on sunglasses." or "It is raining. Open an umbrella."). The teacher reads the instruction aloud, and students point to the corresponding picture or do a simple TPR action. Then, the teacher shows the instruction and asks volunteers to read it themselves and perform the action, emphasizing the reading part.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Read and Follow Directions" Worksheet</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides each student with a worksheet titled "My Weather Day!" The worksheet contains a scene with empty spaces or outlines of simple weather elements (e.g., a blank sky, a child's outline). Below the scene, there are 3-4 simple, one-step written directions, accompanied by small visuals as support (e.g., "Draw a sun.", "Color the clouds blue.", "Circle the rain.", "Write 'windy' here."). Students read each direction independently and perform the action on their worksheet. This task allows them to demonstrate their ability to read and interpret short, written instructions with visual aid, directly linking to the specific objective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are actively engaging with weather-related vocabulary and simple sentences (present continuous and subject + verb + adjective) by responding to instructions about "It's Raining" and "Nice Weather Today!" in a practical, hands-on way.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the "Read and Follow Directions" activity and collection of the completed worksheets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete the "My Weather Day!" worksheet by reading and following the written directions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies weather-related words in instructions (e.g., distinguishes "sun" from "cloud").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately matches simple words to corresponding pictures or actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successfully follows one-step written directions (e.g., draws a sun when instructed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates recognition of initial phonemes (/s/ in sunny, /r/ in rainy) when reading or writing words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show a thumbs up or thumbs down for two statements: "I can read the word 'sun'." and "I can read the word 'rain'."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What weather word did you like to read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the written instructions?" (Students can point to a happy face or a sad face on the board).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to match words to pictures and follow one-step directions with visual support. The phonemic awareness practice for /s/ and /r/ was successful through repetition and visual cues. Some students found the longer instructions slightly challenging, indicating a need for more direct modeling and simple sentence structure practice. For the next lesson, I will ensure continued emphasis on reading simple sentences and introduce speaking opportunities using these read words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson provided a strong foundation for identifying weather words in text. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use these newly read words to describe weather conditions and ask basic questions, building on their reading recognition to produce oral language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>